    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background and objectives",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-1-background-and-objectives.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES\nBackground and objectives\n1 \neport\nR\n1.1\n1.2\n1 First Meeting of the Global Working Group on TB/HIV, 9-11 April 2001, Geneva, Switzerland.\nWHO/CDS/TB/2001.293\nSignificant changes in the policy environment that took place between the meetings\nincluded:\n• In April 2001, the UN Secretary General issued a call to action for the creation of \na Global Fund to fight HIV/AIDS, TB and Malaria (GFATM). Donor pledges to this \nfund have led to it becoming potentially a major new source of support for action \nagainst TB/HIV.  Amongst the countries represented at the Nairobi worksho",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes policy changes between the two meetings, including the creation of the Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria, to which several countries had applied, and grants of TB drugs to 23 countries by the Global TB Drug Facility.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Objectives",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-13-objectives.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The objectives of the second meeting were as follows.\n• To determine progress with joint TB and HIV activities and identify opportunities \nand constraints at international and national levels.\n• To produce practical recommendations for the expansion of joint actions against \nTB/HIV by TB and HIV/AIDS programmes.\n• To share experiences (at country and regional level) regarding both the \nprevalence of TB/HIV coinfection and efforts (both ProTEST and other activities) \nto control the overlapping epidemics of TB and HIV.\n• To identify ways of shifting the focus of the working group from global str",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the meeting's objectives: reviewing progress with joint TB and HIV activities, making practical recommendations, sharing country experience, shifting the group's focus to country implementation, and finalising the collaborative activity guidelines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Country presentations",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-21-country-presentations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Senegal \nDr Mandiaye Loume (Director of Health, Ministry of Health and Prevention\nSenegal) described efforts to integrate TB and AIDS control efforts. There have been\nan estimated 30,000 AIDS deaths in Senegal. HIV prevalence is estimated at 1.4%. \nTwo governing bodies were established to deal with HIV/AIDS: the National AIDS\nControl Committee (NACC) and the National AIDS Control Programme (NACP). The\nNACC has now been replaced by the National AIDS Control Council, headed by the\nPrime Minister. The NACP has sections for epidemic surveillance, diagnosis and\ncounselling, and treatment. Triple AR",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises country presentations on TB/HIV from Senegal, Nigeria, Malawi and Cambodia, covering HIV prevalence, TB burden and treatment outcomes, programme structures and plans for collaboration. Plans mentioned include expanding counselling and testing, home-based care and preventive therapy.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Presentations from international organizations",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-22-presentations-from-international-organization.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The World Bank\nDr K Hansen, Manager of the World Bank AIDS Campaign Team for\nAfrica, outlined the position of TB/HIV in the Bank’s Multi-country AIDS Programme\nfor Africa (MAP). US$1bn is available to scale up and intensify existing activities; 15\ncountries will be receiving $517m and programmes for a further 12 countries are\nalready in preparation. The Bank is thinking in terms of multi-sectoral, multi-\nstakeholder, long-term efforts that will strengthen the impact of AIDS control\nprogrammes and mitigate the impact of the disease in all sectors.\nMAP supports capacity building, expansion of re",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises presentations from international organisations, including the World Bank's Multi-country AIDS Programme for Africa, UNAIDS advocacy and regional support, and USAID funding for TB/HIV. It also covers WHO regional offices, the WHO HIV/AIDS department and research on causes of death among people with HIV. A study is cited showing that cryptococcosis was the most common cause of death while TB caused few, and the section concludes that effective TB control offers the best immediate chance to improve survival.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "ProTEST",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-31-protest.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A summary\nDr Philip Onyebujoh, from Stop TB, WHO, looked at achievements made\nso far in the ProTEST projects, supported by WHO, USAID and CDC-GAP.  The\nstrategies of ProTEST are: to reduce HIV transmission through quality VCT services,\npromotion of condoms, and, where possible, to treatment of STIs; to reduce TB\ntransmission through active case finding (ACF) and enhanced case-holding; to\nreduce TB reactivation through appropriate use of preventive therapy; to link HIV\nprevention services more closely to HIV care; and to increase collaboration between\nTB and HIV stakeholders.\nThe ProTEST projec",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises achievements of the ProTEST projects in South Africa, Malawi and Zambia, which increased uptake of HIV counselling and testing and isoniazid preventive therapy but had poor adherence. It lists next steps, including phased implementation proposals from eight countries.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Progress with joint TB/HIV interventions",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-3-progress-with-joint-tbhiv-interventions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "PROGRESS WITH JOINT TB/HIV INTERVENTIONS\nProgress with joint TB/HIV \ninterventions\n3 \n3.1\n3.1.1\nwith programmes supported by other agencies such as GFATM, CDC and USAID;\nthe holding of implementation workshops; capacity development; the standardisation\nof indicators; improved monitoring and evaluation; and the provision of technical\nassistance to countries implementing joint TB and HIV/AIDS programme activities.\nWHO/HQ will work with WHO, AFRO in bringing francophone countries into the PIA\nstrategy.\nFormal evaluation\nDr Peter Godfrey-Faussett and Dr Lilani Kumaranayake, both of the\nLondon Scho",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines further next steps for phased implementation and describes the formal evaluation of ProTEST by the London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine, including cost-effectiveness analyses and behaviour surveys.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Country experiences",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-32-country-experiences.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Ethiopia\nDr Berhane Kidane-Mariam (TB and Leprosy Programme Manager,\nEthiopia) spoke on TB/HIV collaborative efforts in Ethiopia, where only 52% of the\n65 million population are covered by health services. The estimated incidence of TB\nSECOND MEETING OF THE GLOBAL WORKING GROUP ON TB/HIV\n22\n3.1.2\n3.2\n3.2.1\nis 260/100,000 (all forms) and 109/100,000 (smear-positive). HIV prevalence stands\nat around 7%. There have been 105,781 reported AIDS cases. An estimated\n960,000 people are coinfected with TB/HIV. As regards treatment outcome, in 2000\nthe success rate for new sm+ PTB patients was 78% (SCC),",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports country experiences of joint TB/HIV activities in Ethiopia, Kenya, South Africa and other countries, with data on TB incidence, HIV prevalence, treatment success and programme challenges. It describes efforts to decentralise DOTS, expand testing and preventive therapy, and coordinate with partners. The section ends with break-out group conclusions that scaling up requires better staffing, adherence to prophylaxis and community involvement.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Presentation of the guidelines",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-41-presentation-of-the-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Dr Fabio Scano (Stop TB Department, WHO) reminded participants of the\nphilosophy of the global strategic framework to decrease the burden of TB/HIV: TB\nand HIV/AIDS programmes had largely pursued separate courses, despite\noverlapping TB/HIV epidemiology, but now there was a shift to a strengthened\nunified health sector strategy to control TB/HIV as an integral part of the response to\nHIV/AIDS. This new approach would involve: full implementation of the DOTS\nstrategy; additional interventions against TB (including intensified case-finding and\nTB preventive treatment); interventions to decrease",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recalls the philosophy of the global TB/HIV framework, a shift from separate programmes to a unified strategy combining DOTS with TB prevention, HIV prevention and care for co-infected people.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "activities",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-activities.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "health districts, whilst recognising the need for reducing the burden of the two\noverlapping epidemics, may not be aware of potential collaborative activities and\ntheir benefits. The guidelines are intended for district policy-makers and health\nproviders (for whom they need to be simple, practical, evidenced-based and\nadaptable, to enable rational planning and priority setting) and national policy makers\nand programme managers (who need information for policy consensus and strategy,\nadvocacy and technical assistance). Collaborative TB and HIV programme activities\nshould build on TB and HIV int",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the draft guidelines for collaborative TB/HIV activities, their target audiences of district and national decision-makers, the steps they recommend starting with a situation analysis, and the timetable for finalising them and developing training tools.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Discussion",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-42-discussion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Participants were agreed that the first volume of the guidelines would\nmake a valuable contribution to joint TB/HIV activities. (This volume provides\nbackground information and deals with the planning and implementation of joint\nprogrammes.)  Discussion largely focused on the usefulness of the second volume,\nwhich dealt with the evidence and experience base (from ProTEST and other\nsources) that was used to compile the activity guidelines. A range of views was\nexpressed. Some participants considered it to be useful and appropriate for\ninclusion in the guidelines as an annex. Others, especially",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports discussion of the guidelines, in which participants valued the first volume but disagreed on whether the second, evidence-based volume should be an annex or separate document. It was agreed to finish the guidelines within three months and make them simpler.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Experience in Brazil",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-51-experience-in-brazil.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Dr Marco Vitória of the Brazilian STD/AIDS Programme described Brazil’s\naction against HIV/AIDS, focusing on strategies implemented to provide universal,\nfree anti-retroviral therapy to people living with HIV. This initiative began in 1991, with\nlimited distribution of ZDV capsules, and was extended in 1996, when a presidential\ndecree guaranteed free access to essential medication to all HIV-infected individuals.\nThe Ministry of Health has set up three AIDS task forces, which keep treatment\ncriteria under review in the light of new medical advances. Specific guidelines for\nTB/HIV coinfection h",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes Brazil's provision of free universal antiretroviral therapy since 1996, with about 113 000 people treated by 2001, low primary drug resistance and estimated savings of US$1.8 billion on hospital and outpatient care.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Anti-retroviral treatment (ART)",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-5-anti-retroviral-treatment-art.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "ANTI-RETROVIRAL TREATMENT (ART)\nAnti-retroviral treatment \n(ART)\n5 \n5.1\nThe Brazilian experience demonstrates that effective AIDS treatment is feasible in a\nmiddle-income country - Brazil’s GNP per capita for 1999 was $4790.  It has been\nshown that it can alter the natural history of coinfections, including TB.  Whether the\nsame results can be repeated in countries with significantly higher HIV prevalence\nrates, and lower incomes, remains to be seen.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that Brazil's experience shows AIDS treatment can work in a middle-income country, but questions whether this can be repeated in poorer countries with higher HIV prevalence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Experience in Malawi",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-52-experience-in-malawi.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The presentation of  Dr A Harries (see 3.2.4) included an account of\nMalawi’s experience with the use of ART. Challenges include safeguarding the drug\nsupply and ensuring routine laboratory monitoring. The criteria for starting ART are\ncrucial: too late in the decline of immune function and it will prevent little TB. If ART\nstarts two months after TB treatment, it will not address the high percentage of TB\ndeaths that occur in the first two months. There is a need for demonstration projects\nat district and country level, and for operational research to determine whether\nimmediate joint treatme",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises Malawi's experience with antiretroviral therapy, highlighting drug supply, laboratory monitoring and the timing of treatment relative to TB therapy, and calls for demonstration projects and operational research.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
