    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "WHO guidelines",
      "pageStart": 35,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-53-who-guidelines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Dr Charles Gilks (Department of HIV/AIDS/STIs, WHO) gave an overview\nof WHO’s recently published guidelines on the use of ART in resource-constrained\ncountries.1 Primary audiences for the guidelines are national treatment advisory\nboards, AIDS programme managers, and other senior policymakers. They are not\nintended to be a clinical manual for patient management. \nKey considerations in the development of the guidelines were:\n• the need for potent regimens (including at least three drugs) to prevent \nresistance and maximise benefit \n• standardisation to allow use in settings where HIV/AIDS speci",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Outlines WHO's new guidelines for antiretroviral therapy in resource-limited settings, aimed at policymakers, stressing potent three-drug regimens, standardisation, evidence-based recommendations and flexibility.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-018",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Discussion",
      "pageStart": 36,
      "pageEnd": 38,
      "target": "../sections/sec-018-54-discussion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The discussion that concluded this session centred on the WHO\nguidelines for ART. Participants expressed the need for a sharing of national and\nregional experience in adapting the guidelines to local situations. The need for\ntraining health workers in the use of ART was also stressed; WHO is already\nengaged in the development of guidelines. \nParticipants concluded that the data from Brazil (especially when combined with a\nSouth African report of an 80% reduction in TB incidence with the use of HAART2) -\nprovided ample evidence to justify efforts to increase access to ART. The WG\nshould, theref",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports discussion of the WHO antiretroviral guidelines, with calls for sharing adaptation experience and training health workers, and the conclusion that evidence from Brazil and South Africa justifies including HAART in joint TB/HIV activities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-019",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "The impact of available interventions",
      "pageStart": 38,
      "pageEnd": 38,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-61-the-impact-of-available-interventions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Christine Currie (Faculty of Mathematics, University of Southampton, UK)\npresented an epidemiological model of TB and HIV, and discussed its possible\napplication. There are two sub-models: one for people who are HIV negative or at an\nearly stage of the HIV disease, and one for people who are HIV-positive or are at a\nlater stage of the disease. The model can be used to explore the impact of a range\nof different interventions against TB and HIV. Data from the current situation in\nKenya was used to illustrate how it can be used in practice.  The interventions so far\nmodelled are: \n• Improving TB",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes a mathematical model of TB and HIV, illustrated with data from Kenya, used to explore the impact of interventions such as better TB cure and detection, latent TB treatment and antiretroviral therapy on TB incidence.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
