    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-021",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Implications of an analysis of the TB and HIV epidemics",
      "pageStart": 39,
      "pageEnd": 41,
      "target": "../sections/sec-021-62-implications-of-an-analysis-of-the-tb-and-hiv.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "epidemics\nDr Brian Williams (Stop TB, WHO) stressed the importance of taking a dynamic view\nof epidemics and for a broader perspective on the nature of TB/HIV interactions. In\nmany East and southern African countries it seems clear that even reasonably good\nDOTS programmes are in themselves not  sufficient to bring the rising epidemic of\nTB under control. Various other interventions which, if combined with DOTS, may\nsignificantly reduce the future burden of TB include: \n• the provision of TB preventive therapy for those with HIV;\n• TB mass treatment in situations where the incidence of TB is v",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises an analysis of TB and HIV epidemic dynamics arguing that DOTS alone cannot control rising TB in East and southern Africa. It discusses added interventions such as preventive therapy, mass treatment and wide ART provision. Country projections suggest that only rapid DOTS expansion reduces TB, though the forecasts are uncertain.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-022",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Discussion",
      "pageStart": 41,
      "pageEnd": 42,
      "target": "../sections/sec-022-63-discussion.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "It was agreed that the presentations demonstrated the need for global and\nstrategic thinking and for regular monitoring of both the TB and the HIV epidemics.\nNational rates may be misleading given that there are very substantial intra-country\nvariations in both TB and HIV, and ranking countries simply according to their\nnational figures could in some cases be misleading. Given the size of some of the\nstates in India, for example, it will be important to collect and analyse data on a\nsubstantially finer geographical scale. \nClearly, the impact of ART on TB and other opportunistic infections wil",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports discussion of the modelling, stressing regular monitoring of both epidemics at finer geographical scales and the importance of when ART is started. It lists conclusions, including a step-wise approach to scaling up and attention to human resources and management.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
