    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-024",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Advocacy and Information, Education & Communication",
      "pageStart": 43,
      "pageEnd": 43,
      "target": "../sections/sec-024-72-advocacy-and-information-education-communicat.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Communication (Group 3)\nAdvocacy and IEC both have a vital part to play in addressing TB/HIV. However,\nrelatively few activities have yet been conducted and it is hard at this stage to make\nconcrete recommendations. More needs to be done to establish what actually\nworks.\nAdvocacy and IEC activities must articulate the problems and specify the targets in a\nsimple manner. Memorable slogans must be found!\nAdvocacy is needed to win political commitment. An important argument is that TB\ntreatment for PLWHA is the most affordable and cost-effective available intervention\nfor cutting AIDS mortality r",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the advocacy and communication group's recommendations, such as simple messages, promoting TB treatment as a cost-effective way to cut AIDS deaths, and targeting audiences from UN theme groups and the Global Fund to health workers and communities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Surveillance, monitoring and evaluation",
      "pageStart": 43,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-73-surveillance-monitoring-and-evaluation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "It was noted that collection of data had been stressed throughout the draft\nguidelines for collaborative TB/HIV activities. Data are required for effective\nprogrammes, and quality information can help win political commitment. The WG\nmade the following recommendations.\n• Indicators must be agreed to help set objectives and goals. WHO’s work to \nidentify suitable indicators must be completed as soon as possible and shared \nwith partners.\n• Surveillance should be systematic and regular. Data collected must be \ninterpreted and the findings disseminated. It is important that service providers \nrec",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives recommendations on surveillance, monitoring and evaluation, including agreed indicators, regular surveillance with feedback, measuring HIV prevalence among all TB patients and setting level-specific targets.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Provision and improvement of clinical care",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 45,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-74-provision-and-improvement-of-clinical-care.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Diagnosis (both of TB and of HIV) must be strengthened in general health\nservices, and at TB, HIV and VCT treatment sites. This will require the following.\n• Strengthening the health system, particularly at district level. District health \nmanagement should play a more central role. Collaboration between medical and \nother sectors should be improved. \n• Acting in the community to increase awareness of signs/symptoms and \nencourage seeking treatment. Stigma against both conditions should be \naddressed. Traditional healers and pharmacy outlets should be included in \ncommunity action plans.\n• Rem",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends strengthening TB and HIV diagnosis and clinical care through district health systems, community action against stigma, removing access barriers, better staffing and linking antiretroviral programmes with TB programmes.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Scaling up joint activities",
      "pageStart": 45,
      "pageEnd": 46,
      "target": "../sections/sec-027-75-scaling-up-joint-activities.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "(Group 6)\n• The WG urged WHO and partners to continue and expand their work to establish \nthe evidence base for TB/HIV interventions through phased implementation of \nnationally defined core activities.\n• Joint activities will vary but the following must always be considered for inclusion: \nsyndromic management of sexually transmitted infections, voluntary counselling \nand testing for HIV, active case finding, isoniazid preventive treatment, \ncotrimoxazole preventive treatment, treatment of opportunistic infections, home-\nbased care, prevention of mother to child transmission, anti-retroviral",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Recommends expanding the evidence base through phased implementation and lists core joint activities to consider, such as counselling and testing, preventive therapies and antiretroviral treatment. It stresses starting with a national situation analysis and protecting core DOTS programmes during scale-up.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-67839/blocks/prose-sec-028",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Summary of key recommendations",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 55,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-76-summary-of-key-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "1. In recognition that control of TB in high HIV prevalence settings requires full\nimplementation of the DOTS strategy and incorporation of additional joint TB/HIV\nactivities, the WG urges WHO: \n• to submit a resolution to the WHA in 2003 that urges governments to\nimplement an HIV/AIDS care strategy and country-level implementation of joint \nTB/HIV activities, as set out in the Global TB /HIV Strategic Framework\n• that the HIV/AIDS Department should finalise the development of its AIDS care \nstrategy and, without delay, share details of the principles on which it is based \n• that the Stop TB D",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarises the working group's key recommendations, including a proposed 2003 World Health Assembly resolution on joint TB/HIV activities, finalising the guidelines and an AIDS care strategy, and actions on funding, research and capacity. It ends with the meeting agenda (Annex 1) and the heading of the participant list (Annex 2).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
